1
00:00:07,180 --> 00:00:13,700
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,
the dear son of Odysseus rose up from his bed,

2
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:17,600
put on his clothes, slung his
sharp sword about his shoulder,

3
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:23,820
bound the beautiful sandals beneath his shining feet,
and came out of his bedroom looking like a god.

4
00:00:23,820 --> 00:00:29,360
At once he ordered the clear-voiced heralds
to cry the long-haired Achaeans into assembly.

5
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:33,440
The heralds cried the summons,
and the men gathered quickly.

6
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:39,380
And when they were assembled, all in one gathering,
he went to the assembly, the bronze spear in his palm,

7
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:45,600
not alone: two swift dogs followed with him,
and Athena poured a wondrous grace upon him.

8
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:52,820
All the people gazed at him in wonder as he came;
he sat in his father's seat, and the elders gave way.

9
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:59,960
Then the hero Aegyptius was first to speak among them,
a man bent with age, who knew things past numbering.

10
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:06,260
His own dear son had gone with godlike Odysseus
in the hollow ships to Ilium of the fine horses —

11
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:13,120
Antiphus the spearman; the savage Cyclops killed him
in his hollow cave, and made of him his last supper.

12
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:17,720
Three other sons he had:
one kept company with the suitors —

13
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:20,480
Eurynomus — and two worked their father's lands always.

14
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:24,440
Even so, he could not forget the one:
grieving, mourning him.

15
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:27,980
In tears for him, he now addressed the assembly:

16
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:31,120
"Hear me now, men of Ithaca, hear what I say.

17
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:37,300
Never once has our assembly met, has our council sat,
since noble Odysseus went away in the hollow ships.

18
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:44,320
Who has gathered us now? On whom has need come down so hard,
whether among the young men or the elder-born?

19
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:50,060
Has he heard some news of the army coming home which
he might tell us plainly, having learned of it first?

20
00:01:50,060 --> 00:01:53,440
Or is there some other public
matter he would open and argue?

21
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:57,300
A good man he seems to me, a blessed one. May Zeus

22
00:01:57,300 --> 00:02:01,340
accomplish for him some good —
whatever his heart desires."

23
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:06,020
So he spoke, and the dear son of Odysseus
rejoiced at the omen of the word,

24
00:02:06,020 --> 00:02:09,200
and sat no longer: desire took him to speak.

25
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:12,600
He stood in the middle of the assembly,
and the scepter was put in his hand

26
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:16,440
by the herald Peisenor, a man of clear-headed counsel.

27
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:19,940
Then he spoke, turning first to the old man:

28
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:24,840
"Old sir, that man is not far off —
you shall see for yourself —

29
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:29,980
who called the people: it is I;
grief comes down hardest on me.

30
00:02:29,980 --> 00:02:35,840
I have heard no news of the army coming home which I
might tell you plainly, having learned of it first,

31
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:39,260
nor is there other public matter I would open and argue:

32
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:46,280
it is my own need, the evils fallen on my house —
twofold. I have lost my noble father, who once was king

33
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:52,440
among you here, and he was gentle as a father;
and now something far greater, which will soon tear apart

34
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:56,720
my whole house utterly and destroy my living altogether.

35
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:03,080
Suitors have set upon my mother — against her will —
the own dear sons of the men who here are noblest,

36
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:06,340
and they shrink from going to the house of her father

37
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:09,680
Icarius, so that he might set his
daughter's bride-terms himself

38
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:13,380
and give her to whom he will,
whoever comes and pleases her.

39
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:18,580
Instead they haunt our house day after day,
slaughtering our oxen and our sheep and our fat goats,

40
00:03:18,580 --> 00:03:20,500
feasting and drinking the fire-bright wine

41
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:25,280
with no reckoning. And the most of it
is wasted. For there is no man here

42
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:29,000
such as Odysseus was, to beat ruin away from the house.

43
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,520
We are not the men to beat it
back — believe me, ever after

44
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:35,460
we would be pitiable men, and unschooled in fighting.

45
00:03:35,460 --> 00:03:38,520
Beat it back I would, if only the strength were in me!

46
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:45,820
For what is done here is past all bearing; past all
decency my house is ruined. Feel the outrage yourselves;

47
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:48,420
feel shame before the peoples who are our neighbors,

48
00:03:48,420 --> 00:03:52,400
those who dwell around us;
and dread the wrath of the gods,

49
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:56,120
lest in fury at evil deeds they
turn them back on your heads.

50
00:03:56,120 --> 00:04:02,380
I beg you — by Olympian Zeus, and by Themis,
who dissolves the assemblies of men and seats them —

51
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:09,680
hold off, friends! Leave me alone to waste away in my
bitter grief. Unless perhaps my father, good Odysseus,

52
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:15,000
in malice did the strong-greaved Achaeans harm,
and in requital you do me harm in malice,

53
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,940
egging these men on. Better for me by far if it were
you yourselves devouring my treasures and my herds:

54
00:04:20,940 --> 00:04:24,560
were you the ones eating,
there might one day be repayment,

55
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:27,480
for then we would go up and down
the town pressing our claim,

56
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:30,800
demanding our goods back, until all was given over.

57
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:34,120
Instead you heap on my heart pains past any remedy."

58
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:37,980
So he spoke in anger,
and dashed the scepter to the ground,

59
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:42,000
bursting into tears. And pity took hold of all the people.

60
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,860
Then all the rest stayed silent, and no one
dared to answer Telemachus with hard words;

61
00:04:47,860 --> 00:04:49,740
Antinous alone made him this answer:

62
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:54,620
"Telemachus, high talker, temper past
restraining — what a thing you have said,

63
00:04:54,620 --> 00:04:58,100
smearing us with shame!
You would love to hang the blame on us.

64
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:00,720
But it is not the Achaean suitors who are at fault:

65
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:04,420
it is your own dear mother, whose cunning has no equal.

66
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:07,220
For it is the third year now, and the fourth soon coming,

67
00:05:07,220 --> 00:05:10,080
that she has cheated the heart in the Achaeans' breasts.

68
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:13,620
She gives hope to all, and makes promises to every man,

69
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:16,920
sending out messages — while her mind wills otherwise.

70
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:19,300
And here is another trick her heart devised:

71
00:05:19,300 --> 00:05:22,300
she set up a great loom in the halls and wove at it,

72
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:25,920
fine-threaded, beyond all measure wide,
and told us straightway:

73
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:31,760
'Young men, my suitors — since noble Odysseus is dead,
wait, though you burn for my wedding, till I finish

74
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:37,480
this cloth — let my spinning not be wasted on the wind —
a shroud for the hero Laertes, against the day

75
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:40,760
the ruinous doom of death that lays men low will take him:

76
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:43,180
let no Achaean woman in the land hold it against me

77
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:46,640
that a man who won so much should
lie without his winding-sheet.'

78
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:49,540
So she spoke, and our proud hearts were persuaded.

79
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:54,240
Then every day she wove at the great loom,
and every night she unwove it by torchlight.

80
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:58,180
Three years her trick escaped us,
and she kept the Achaeans persuaded;

81
00:05:58,180 --> 00:06:03,700
but when the fourth year came, as the seasons rolled on,
then one of her women, who knew it all, told us,

82
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:06,040
and we caught her unweaving the shining web.

83
00:06:06,040 --> 00:06:09,380
So she finished it — against her will, under force.

84
00:06:09,380 --> 00:06:12,080
Now the suitors give you this answer,
so that you may know it

85
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:14,680
in your own heart, and all the Achaeans know it:

86
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:19,580
send your mother away. Command her to marry
whichever man her father bids, and who pleases her.

87
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:22,560
But if she goes on tormenting the sons of the Achaeans —

88
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:27,960
minding in her heart the gifts Athena gave her beyond
all women, skill in beautiful work, and a noble mind,

89
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:31,580
and cunning past anything we
hear of even in the women of old,

90
00:06:31,580 --> 00:06:37,720
the fair-braided Achaean women of the time before,
Tyro and Alcmene and Mycene of the lovely crown:

91
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:42,900
not one of them had thoughts to match Penelope's —
yet this one thought of hers was not well thought.

92
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:46,060
For just so long will men devour
your living and your goods

93
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:50,700
as she holds to this purpose the gods are setting
now in her breast. Great fame she is making

94
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:53,680
for herself — and for you,
the ache of a squandered living.

95
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:59,600
We are not going to our estates, or anywhere else,
before she marries whichever Achaean she will."

96
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:02,600
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

97
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:05,340
"Antinous, there is no way I can
thrust out of the house, unwilling,

98
00:07:05,340 --> 00:07:09,080
the one who bore me, who raised me —
while my father, somewhere on earth,

99
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:12,580
lives or is dead. And it would go hard with me to pay back

100
00:07:12,580 --> 00:07:15,960
Icarius so much, if of my own
accord I send my mother home.

101
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:18,660
From her father I would suffer evil; and heaven would add

102
00:07:18,660 --> 00:07:24,100
more, since my mother, leaving the house, would call
down the loathed Furies; and the blame of men would be

103
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:27,380
upon me. No: I will never speak that word.

104
00:07:27,380 --> 00:07:30,840
As for you — if your own hearts feel any shame at it,

105
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:34,440
get out of my halls. Go busy yourselves with other feasts,

106
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:37,260
eating your own goods, moving from house to house.

107
00:07:37,260 --> 00:07:40,160
But if this seems to you the
better thing, the more profitable,

108
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:43,740
that one man's living be destroyed and nothing paid,

109
00:07:43,740 --> 00:07:47,140
then carve away. And I will cry
to the gods who live forever,

110
00:07:47,140 --> 00:07:50,560
in hope that Zeus may grant
deeds of requital come to pass:

111
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:53,840
then you would die inside this house,
and nothing paid for you."

112
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:59,280
So spoke Telemachus. And Zeus of
the wide voice sent him two eagles,

113
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:02,320
flying from on high, from a mountain peak.

114
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:08,560
For a while the two flew on with the breath of the wind,
close by one another, wings at full stretch;

115
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:11,000
but when they came dead over the many-voiced assembly,

116
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:14,660
they wheeled there, beating their wings thick and fast,

117
00:08:14,660 --> 00:08:18,860
and glared down on all their heads,
and death was in their gaze;

118
00:08:18,860 --> 00:08:22,060
then they ripped with their talons
at each other's cheeks and throats

119
00:08:22,060 --> 00:08:25,420
and shot away rightward, over the houses and the town.

120
00:08:25,420 --> 00:08:28,820
The men were astonished at the birds their eyes had seen,

121
00:08:28,820 --> 00:08:32,500
and turned over in their hearts
what this would bring to pass.

122
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:38,640
And among them spoke the old hero Halitherses,
Mastor's son, who surpassed all men of his day

123
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:42,120
in reading birds and speaking what fate intends.

124
00:08:42,120 --> 00:08:44,440
With good will toward them he spoke to the assembly:

125
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:50,440
"Hear me now, men of Ithaca, hear what I say —
though to the suitors above all I declare it:

126
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:53,800
on them a great wave of ruin is rolling. For Odysseus

127
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:57,040
will not be long parted from
his people; already, somewhere

128
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:00,620
near, he is planting death and slaughter for these men,

129
00:09:00,620 --> 00:09:07,340
every one; and evil will come on many of the rest of
us who live in clear-skied Ithaca. Long before that,

130
00:09:07,340 --> 00:09:13,280
let us think how to stop them — or let them stop themselves:
the sooner the better, for their own sake.

131
00:09:13,280 --> 00:09:16,540
I prophesy as no novice; I know my art.

132
00:09:16,540 --> 00:09:22,340
And for that man too I say it is all coming home,
all I told him when the Argives went up into Ilium

133
00:09:22,340 --> 00:09:25,040
and Odysseus of the many designs went with them.

134
00:09:25,040 --> 00:09:31,020
I said that after long suffering, his companions all lost,
unknown to everyone, in the twentieth year

135
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:34,420
he would come home. And now it is all being accomplished."

136
00:09:34,420 --> 00:09:37,220
Then Eurymachus, Polybus' son, answered him:

137
00:09:37,220 --> 00:09:41,340
"Old man, off with you —
go home and prophesy to your children,

138
00:09:41,340 --> 00:09:43,880
lest some evil overtake them in days to come.

139
00:09:43,880 --> 00:09:46,540
In this I am a far better prophet than you.

140
00:09:46,540 --> 00:09:49,600
Plenty of birds go back and forth
beneath the rays of the sun,

141
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:52,540
and not all of them are fate. As for Odysseus:

142
00:09:52,540 --> 00:09:56,440
he is dead, far away — and how I
wish that you had perished with him.

143
00:09:56,440 --> 00:10:00,920
You would not then be spouting all this prophecy,
or whetting the anger of Telemachus —

144
00:10:00,920 --> 00:10:04,320
angling for some gift for your
household, should he offer one.

145
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:07,340
But I will tell you this, and it will be accomplished:

146
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:10,160
if you, who know so many things grown old with you,

147
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:13,140
talk a younger man around with words and stir him to fury,

148
00:10:13,140 --> 00:10:16,000
for the man himself, first, it will go the harder,

149
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:19,540
and in any case he will accomplish
nothing for all these words;

150
00:10:19,540 --> 00:10:22,480
and on you, old man, we will lay a fine that in the paying

151
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:25,580
will gall your heart: and the pain of it will be hard.

152
00:10:25,580 --> 00:10:29,040
And to Telemachus, before you all, my own advice is this:

153
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:32,200
let him command his mother to
go back to her father's house.

154
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:35,460
There they will make the marriage
and set out the bride-gifts,

155
00:10:35,460 --> 00:10:38,580
very many, all that should follow a beloved child.

156
00:10:38,580 --> 00:10:40,940
Not before, I think, will the sons of the Achaeans quit

157
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:45,640
their courting — a grinding courtship.
We fear no one, not at all;

158
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:51,500
no, not Telemachus, for all his stream of words;
nor do we care a whit for prophecy, old man, that you

159
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:55,060
mouth to no accomplishment —
it only makes you the more hated.

160
00:10:55,060 --> 00:11:01,240
His goods will be devoured as badly as ever, and never
made good, so long as she puts the Achaeans off about

161
00:11:01,240 --> 00:11:07,000
her marriage. And we, waiting all our days,
rival one another for her excellence, and do not go after

162
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,980
other women, fit brides for any of us."

163
00:11:09,980 --> 00:11:13,720
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

164
00:11:13,720 --> 00:11:15,940
"Eurymachus, and all you other haughty suitors:

165
00:11:15,940 --> 00:11:19,540
I plead with you no further; I say no more of this.

166
00:11:19,540 --> 00:11:22,700
The gods know it now, and all the Achaeans.

167
00:11:22,700 --> 00:11:28,360
But come, give me a fast ship and twenty companions
to carry me on a journey there and back again.

168
00:11:28,360 --> 00:11:34,840
For I am going to Sparta and to sandy Pylos to
ask after the homecoming of my long-gone father,

169
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:38,160
in hope some mortal man will tell me,
or I may hear the Rumor

170
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:42,500
that comes from Zeus, which most
of all carries word among men.

171
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:48,720
If I hear my father is alive and homeward bound, then,
ground down as I am, I could hold out one more year;

172
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:54,020
but if I hear that he is dead, that he is no more,
then I will come back to my own dear fatherland

173
00:11:54,020 --> 00:11:57,440
and heap his burial mound,
and over it pay the death-rites,

174
00:11:57,440 --> 00:12:01,200
very many, as is fitting,
and give my mother to a husband."

175
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:06,880
So he spoke, and sat down. And there rose among
them Mentor, the companion of blameless Odysseus,

176
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:10,160
to whom, going aboard his ships,
he had entrusted his whole house —

177
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:13,840
that all should obey the old man,
and he keep everything safe.

178
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:16,700
With good will toward them he spoke to the assembly:

179
00:12:16,700 --> 00:12:19,860
"Hear me now, men of Ithaca, hear what I say.

180
00:12:19,860 --> 00:12:25,820
Let no sceptered king ever again be willingly kind
and gentle, or know the right way in his heart;

181
00:12:25,820 --> 00:12:31,240
let him rather be harsh always, and act outrage —
since no one remembers godlike Odysseus

182
00:12:31,240 --> 00:12:34,800
among the people he ruled, and he was gentle as a father.

183
00:12:34,800 --> 00:12:38,060
It is not the lordly suitors I begrudge, not really,

184
00:12:38,060 --> 00:12:41,300
for their deeds of violence
woven in an evil-scheming mind:

185
00:12:41,300 --> 00:12:43,860
they lay their own heads on the wager when they devour

186
00:12:43,860 --> 00:12:47,860
by violence the house of Odysseus,
saying he will come home no more.

187
00:12:47,860 --> 00:12:50,740
It is the rest of the people I
am angry at — the way you all

188
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:56,940
sit there dumb, and never fasten on them with words
and stop them: the suitors few, and you so many."

189
00:12:56,940 --> 00:13:00,400
Then Leocritus, Evenor's son, answered him:

190
00:13:00,400 --> 00:13:04,260
"Mentor, wrecker, brain gone wandering —
what a thing you have said,

191
00:13:04,260 --> 00:13:10,140
urging them to stop us. It is heavy work to fight
with men over a feast — men who outnumber you at that.

192
00:13:10,140 --> 00:13:15,320
Why, if Ithacan Odysseus himself came in on us
and found the lordly suitors feasting in his house

193
00:13:15,320 --> 00:13:20,200
and set his heart on driving them out of the hall,
his wife would have no joy of his coming, however

194
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:23,620
she has ached for him:
right there he would meet an ugly death,

195
00:13:23,620 --> 00:13:27,500
if he fought men who outnumbered him.
What you have said misses the mark.

196
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:31,140
Come now — people, scatter, each man to his own work.

197
00:13:31,140 --> 00:13:35,020
As for this one, Mentor and
Halitherses can speed his journey,

198
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:37,920
being companions of his father's house from the beginning.

199
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:40,940
Though I think he will sit here a
long while yet, gathering his news

200
00:13:40,940 --> 00:13:43,840
in Ithaca; that voyage he will never make."

201
00:13:43,840 --> 00:13:47,280
So he spoke, and dissolved the assembly on the instant.

202
00:13:47,280 --> 00:13:52,780
They scattered, each man to his own house, and the
suitors went off to the house of godlike Odysseus.

203
00:13:52,780 --> 00:13:56,560
But Telemachus walked apart, to the sand-beach of the sea,

204
00:13:56,560 --> 00:14:00,240
washed his hands in the gray
salt water, and prayed to Athena:

205
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:05,840
"Hear me, you who came yesterday, a god, into our
house, and bade me go by ship over the misted sea

206
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:08,920
to ask after the homecoming of my long-gone father.

207
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:15,100
All of it the Achaeans grind to a halt —
the suitors most of all, in their evil overbearing pride."

208
00:14:15,100 --> 00:14:22,340
So he prayed, and Athena came to him, close at hand,
in the likeness of Mentor, both his body and his voice,

209
00:14:22,340 --> 00:14:26,280
and lifting her voice she spoke winged words to him:

210
00:14:26,280 --> 00:14:30,260
"Telemachus, you will be no
coward from this day, and no fool,

211
00:14:30,260 --> 00:14:33,680
if any of your father's brave force is instilled in you —

212
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:37,840
such a man he was for making good both deed and word.

213
00:14:37,840 --> 00:14:41,980
Then this journey of yours will be no
idle errand, and not unaccomplished.

214
00:14:41,980 --> 00:14:45,300
But if you are not his son, his and Penelope's,

215
00:14:45,300 --> 00:14:49,100
then I have no hope you will
accomplish what you set your heart on.

216
00:14:49,100 --> 00:14:52,300
Few sons, in truth, turn out the equals of their fathers:

217
00:14:52,300 --> 00:14:56,160
the most are worse; a few are
better men than their fathers.

218
00:14:56,160 --> 00:14:59,340
But since you will be no coward
from this day, and no fool,

219
00:14:59,340 --> 00:15:05,660
and the cunning of Odysseus has not wholly failed in you,
there is every hope you will accomplish this work.

220
00:15:05,660 --> 00:15:11,700
So now let the suitors' plotting and scheming go —
mindless men, with neither sense nor justice in them:

221
00:15:11,700 --> 00:15:18,120
they know nothing of the death and the black doom that
stands close by them, to destroy them all in a day.

222
00:15:18,120 --> 00:15:21,780
The journey you have set your
heart on will not be long delayed —

223
00:15:21,780 --> 00:15:24,980
such a companion am I, out of your father's house:

224
00:15:24,980 --> 00:15:28,940
I will fit out a fast ship for you, and come along myself.

225
00:15:28,940 --> 00:15:32,540
You go back to the house now, and move among the suitors;

226
00:15:32,540 --> 00:15:35,300
make ready provisions, and stow them all in vessels —

227
00:15:35,300 --> 00:15:42,180
wine in two-handled jars, and barley meal, the marrow
of men, in tight-sewn skins. And I, through the town,

228
00:15:42,180 --> 00:15:46,300
will gather willing companions,
and quickly. There are ships

229
00:15:46,300 --> 00:15:49,700
in plenty in sea-circled Ithaca, new ships and old:

230
00:15:49,700 --> 00:15:55,840
I will look them over for you and choose the best,
and we will rig her fast and launch her on the wide sea."

231
00:15:55,840 --> 00:15:59,720
So spoke Athena, daughter of Zeus. And no longer then

232
00:15:59,720 --> 00:16:03,280
did Telemachus linger, once he
had heard the voice of the god.

233
00:16:03,280 --> 00:16:06,720
He went on his way to the house,
his heart heavy within him,

234
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:12,680
and found the lordly suitors there in the halls,
skinning goats and singeing fat hogs in the courtyard.

235
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:19,000
Antinous came laughing straight at Telemachus,
clamped a hand on his, and spoke a word, and named him:

236
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:23,180
"Telemachus, high talker,
temper past restraining — let no other

237
00:16:23,180 --> 00:16:26,400
evil trouble your breast, no other deed or word.

238
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:30,220
Just eat with me, and drink, the way it used to be.

239
00:16:30,220 --> 00:16:35,440
The Achaeans will see to all those things of yours —
a ship and picked oarsmen — so you may come the sooner

240
00:16:35,440 --> 00:16:39,000
to holy Pylos, after news of your proud father."

241
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,720
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

242
00:16:41,720 --> 00:16:48,140
"Antinous, there is no way, among you in your insolence,
to eat my supper quietly and take my ease in peace.

243
00:16:48,140 --> 00:16:51,340
Is it not enough that all this
while, suitors, you carved up

244
00:16:51,340 --> 00:16:54,980
my goods, so many and so fine, while I was still a child?

245
00:16:54,980 --> 00:17:00,680
Now — when I am grown, and hear the talk of other men
and understand it, and the spirit swells inside me —

246
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:06,120
I will try to hurl an evil doom upon you,
whether I go to Pylos or stay here in this land.

247
00:17:06,120 --> 00:17:10,000
But go I will, and the journey I
speak of will be no idle errand —

248
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,660
a passenger on another's ship,
since master of ship or oarsmen

249
00:17:13,660 --> 00:17:17,540
I am not to be. That, no doubt,
seemed the more profitable to you."

250
00:17:17,540 --> 00:17:20,700
He spoke, and wrenched his hand from Antinous' hand,

251
00:17:20,700 --> 00:17:25,120
easily. And through the house the
suitors busied themselves with the feast,

252
00:17:25,120 --> 00:17:27,460
and they mocked him and needled him with words.

253
00:17:27,460 --> 00:17:30,220
And thus would one of the arrogant young men say:

254
00:17:30,220 --> 00:17:33,280
"No question — Telemachus is planning our murder!

255
00:17:33,280 --> 00:17:39,240
He will bring back avengers from sandy Pylos,
or else from Sparta, since he is so terribly set on it.

256
00:17:39,240 --> 00:17:45,000
Or maybe he means to go to Ephyra, that rich plowland,
to fetch home poisons that eat the life away,

257
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:48,280
and drop them in the wine-bowl, and destroy us all."

258
00:17:48,280 --> 00:17:52,420
And thus would another of the arrogant young men say:

259
00:17:52,420 --> 00:17:55,800
"Who knows? Maybe he too, once aboard the hollow ship,

260
00:17:55,800 --> 00:18:00,640
will wander far from his people
and be lost — just like Odysseus.

261
00:18:00,640 --> 00:18:02,840
And that would double our work, wouldn't it:

262
00:18:02,840 --> 00:18:06,460
we would have to divide all his
goods among us, and the house itself

263
00:18:06,460 --> 00:18:10,140
hand over to his mother — hers,
and the man who marries her."

264
00:18:10,140 --> 00:18:14,120
So they talked. But he went
down to his father's storeroom,

265
00:18:14,120 --> 00:18:17,680
high-ceilinged and broad, where gold and bronze lay piled,

266
00:18:17,680 --> 00:18:24,800
and clothing in chests, and fragrant oil in plenty;
and there stood great jars of old wine, sweet to drink,

267
00:18:24,800 --> 00:18:30,560
holding the unmixed divine drink within them,
ranged in order along the wall — against the day Odysseus

268
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:33,720
might come home even after all his pains.

269
00:18:33,720 --> 00:18:39,740
Doors were on it, close-fitted, bolted fast,
double doors; and a woman-steward was there night and day

270
00:18:39,740 --> 00:18:43,360
who guarded it all with her mind's deep knowledge:

271
00:18:43,360 --> 00:18:46,000
Eurycleia, daughter of Ops, Peisenor's son.

272
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:49,140
Telemachus called her into the storeroom and spoke to her:

273
00:18:49,140 --> 00:18:55,320
"Nurse, come — draw me off wine in two-handled jars,
the sweet wine, the finest-tasting after that

274
00:18:55,320 --> 00:19:00,320
which you keep, thinking of him —
the luckless one — if from somewhere he should come:

275
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:04,300
Zeus-born Odysseus,
having slipped past death and the dooms.

276
00:19:04,300 --> 00:19:06,380
Fill twelve, and fit them all with lids.

277
00:19:06,380 --> 00:19:09,700
And pour me barley meal into well-sewn skins:

278
00:19:09,700 --> 00:19:12,040
twenty measures of mill-crushed barley grain.

279
00:19:12,040 --> 00:19:15,460
Keep this to yourself. And let it all stand gathered:

280
00:19:15,460 --> 00:19:21,240
in the evening I will fetch it, when at last my
mother climbs to her upper room and thinks of sleep.

281
00:19:21,240 --> 00:19:27,940
For I am going to Sparta and to sandy Pylos to ask
after my dear father's homecoming, if I may hear of it."

282
00:19:27,940 --> 00:19:33,920
So he spoke, and his dear nurse Eurycleia wailed aloud,
and grieving she spoke winged words to him:

283
00:19:33,920 --> 00:19:39,560
"Why, dear child, why has this thought come into
your mind? Where would you go across the wide earth,

284
00:19:39,560 --> 00:19:46,400
an only son, and beloved? He is dead far from home —
Zeus-born Odysseus, in some strangers' country.

285
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:50,420
And these men, the moment you are gone,
will plot evils behind you,

286
00:19:50,420 --> 00:19:53,860
to kill you by treachery and
divide all this among themselves.

287
00:19:53,860 --> 00:19:57,300
Stay here instead, sitting on
what is yours. There is no need

288
00:19:57,300 --> 00:20:00,580
to suffer evils on the barren sea, and go wandering."

289
00:20:00,580 --> 00:20:03,220
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

290
00:20:03,220 --> 00:20:06,500
"Take heart, nurse. This plan is not without a god.

291
00:20:06,500 --> 00:20:11,740
But swear you will not breathe a word to my dear mother
before the eleventh day has come, or the twelfth,

292
00:20:11,740 --> 00:20:17,260
or she misses me herself and hears that I am gone —
so she will not mar her lovely skin with weeping."

293
00:20:17,260 --> 00:20:20,960
So he spoke, and the old woman
swore the great oath of the gods.

294
00:20:20,960 --> 00:20:23,520
And when she had sworn and made an end of the oath,

295
00:20:23,520 --> 00:20:29,860
straightway she drew off wine for him in two-handled
jars and poured him barley meal into well-sewn skins;

296
00:20:29,860 --> 00:20:33,480
and Telemachus went back to the
hall to move among the suitors.

297
00:20:33,480 --> 00:20:37,000
Then the goddess thought of one
thing more, gray-eyed Athena.

298
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:40,480
In the likeness of Telemachus she
went everywhere through the town,

299
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:45,960
and standing beside each man she spoke her word,
bidding them gather at evening by the fast ship.

300
00:20:45,960 --> 00:20:51,640
Then she asked Noemon, the shining son of Phronius,
for a fast ship; and he promised it gladly.

301
00:20:51,640 --> 00:20:54,800
The sun went down, and all the ways grew dark.

302
00:20:54,800 --> 00:21:00,360
Then she hauled the fast ship down to the sea,
and stowed in her all the gear that well-benched ships carry,

303
00:21:00,360 --> 00:21:04,180
and moored her at the harbor's edge.
Around her the brave companions

304
00:21:04,180 --> 00:21:07,860
gathered in a body, and the goddess
set each man's heart on fire.

305
00:21:07,860 --> 00:21:11,840
Then the goddess thought of one
thing more, gray-eyed Athena.

306
00:21:11,840 --> 00:21:17,860
She went on her way to the house of godlike Odysseus,
and there she poured sweet sleep over the suitors,

307
00:21:17,860 --> 00:21:21,840
addled them as they drank,
and knocked the cups from their hands.

308
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:27,980
They stumbled off to sleep through the town,
and sat no longer, for sleep was falling on their eyelids.

309
00:21:27,980 --> 00:21:33,040
But to Telemachus gray-eyed Athena spoke,
calling him out in front of the well-settled halls,

310
00:21:33,040 --> 00:21:37,760
in the likeness of Mentor, both his body and his voice:

311
00:21:37,760 --> 00:21:44,080
"Telemachus, your strong-greaved companions are already
sitting at the oars, waiting on your word to start.

312
00:21:44,080 --> 00:21:47,560
Come, let us go; let us not hold the journey long."

313
00:21:47,560 --> 00:21:54,000
So speaking, Pallas Athena led the way swiftly,
and he followed in the footsteps of the god.

314
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:59,040
And when they had come down to the ship and the sea,
they found the long-haired companions on the beach,

315
00:21:59,040 --> 00:22:02,200
and the sacred strength of Telemachus spoke among them:

316
00:22:02,200 --> 00:22:05,840
"Come, friends, let us fetch
the provisions: everything now

317
00:22:05,840 --> 00:22:12,540
stands gathered in the hall. My mother has heard nothing,
nor the slave-women either; one alone was told."

318
00:22:12,540 --> 00:22:15,320
So speaking he led the way, and they went with him.

319
00:22:15,320 --> 00:22:21,560
They carried everything down and stowed it in the
well-benched ship as the dear son of Odysseus commanded.

320
00:22:21,560 --> 00:22:24,820
Then Telemachus went aboard, Athena going before him;

321
00:22:24,820 --> 00:22:28,160
she sat down in the stern of
the ship, and close beside her

322
00:22:28,160 --> 00:22:31,580
sat Telemachus. And the men cast off the stern-lines

323
00:22:31,580 --> 00:22:35,600
and came aboard themselves and
took their places at the oarlocks.

324
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:42,180
And gray-eyed Athena sent them a following wind,
a fresh West Wind, singing over the wine-dark sea.

325
00:22:42,180 --> 00:22:45,040
Telemachus shouted to his companions, commanding them

326
00:22:45,040 --> 00:22:48,080
to lay hands on the rigging, and they heard his urging:

327
00:22:48,080 --> 00:22:52,300
they raised the pine mast and stepped
it inside the hollow mast-box,

328
00:22:52,300 --> 00:22:54,880
set it up and made it fast with the forestays,

329
00:22:54,880 --> 00:22:59,000
and hauled up the white sails by
the well-twisted ox-hide ropes.

330
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:02,120
The wind bellied out the middle of the sail, and the wave

331
00:23:02,120 --> 00:23:08,720
hissed dark and loud around the keel as the ship went on,
and she ran down the waves, making her way.

332
00:23:08,720 --> 00:23:12,020
Then, when they had lashed the
tackle down the fast black ship,

333
00:23:12,020 --> 00:23:18,060
they set up mixing-bowls crowned brim-full with wine
and poured to the deathless gods who are forever,

334
00:23:18,060 --> 00:23:21,160
and beyond all others to the gray-eyed daughter of Zeus.

335
00:23:21,160 --> 00:23:25,010
All night long, and into the dawn, the ship cut her way.
